# Database Normalization

Principle: **one table = one clear business responsibility.**

Normalize around real entities and relationships. Avoid both giant tables and micro-tables for every 2–3 fields.

## Do

- Keep core identity fields on the primary entity (`customers`: name, email, status, timestamps)
- Split repeating or growing data into related tables (`customer_phones`, `job_notes`, `order_items`)
- Use many-to-many join tables for tags/categories when they must be queried
- Use history/status tables when state transitions must be auditable
- Preserve snapshot values on financial/order rows (do not rely only on current product price)
- Prefer JSON only for opaque config, metrics, or provider-specific bags that are not filtered/joined
- Scope uniqueness with `tenant_id` when the business rule is per-tenant: `UNIQUE (tenant_id, sku)`
- Add indexes from real query patterns: `(tenant_id, status)`, `(tenant_id, created_at)`

## Do not

- Put every CRM/commerce/ops field on one wide row
- Create a table for a handful of always-1:1 scalar fields with no independent lifecycle
- Store large binaries in Postgres (use object storage + metadata row)
- Invent commerce/order/inventory schemas before the product needs them
- Over-normalize hot voice-path config (`agent_profiles`) into many joins
- Add indexes “just in case”

## Examples

```text
# ❌ BAD — growing history on the parent row
customers.note1, note2, note3  /  jobs.checklist_item_1…

# ✅ GOOD
customers + customer_notes
jobs + job_notes / job_checklist_items (when items need status/audit)
```

```text
# ❌ BAD — micro-table with no query value
customer_names(first_name, last_name) separate from customers

# ✅ GOOD
customers(first_name, last_name, email, phone, status, created_at)
```

```text
# ❌ BAD — queryable structure buried in JSON forever
customers.tags = '["vip","hvac"]' used in filters

# ✅ GOOD (when filtering/search requires it)
tags + customer_tags
# OK to keep JSON for prompt bags / metrics / audit snapshots
```

## Migrations

- Schema changes go through Alembic; keep models and migrations in sync (including indexes)
- Prefer additive, backward-compatible migrations; do not destroy production data
- For large tables, avoid long exclusive locks; backfill carefully

